# Gate verdicts never travel through a pipe

> **First, the general rule this one is an instance of:** every check must be
> able to fail, and you must be able to name the edit that makes it fail —
> [`50-testing`](50-testing.mdc) §"Every check must be able to fail". A gate
> whose exit code is swallowed is one of seven recorded ways a check ends up
> structurally incapable of going red.


`cmd | tail`, `cmd | grep`, `cmd | head` — the pipeline's exit status is
the LAST command's, so the gate reads the filter's success and the real
verdict is discarded. This has shipped a clippy-red commit and masked a
broken C++ link in the same review round; five instances were tallied in
one SA round alone.

- The verdict command runs **unpiped**: redirect to a log file and read
  the tail afterwards, or capture `$?` on its own line immediately after
  the bare command.
- If a pipe is unavoidable, the verdict is `${PIPESTATUS[0]}` (bash),
  captured on the very next line — not `$?`.
- `set -o pipefail` is correct for **new** gate scripts, with one known
  trap: under pipefail, an early-exiting consumer (`rg -q`, `head`)
  SIGPIPEs its producer and a successful match reads as pipeline
  failure, flaky by input size. Capture into a variable first, then
  match (the SA-3b registry-gate fix). Do not blanket-add pipefail to
  existing scripts without re-checking every pipe for this shape.
- Chains matter: in `gate && commit && push`, a swallowed gate exit
  makes the push unconditional. Anything that publishes (commit, push,
  tag) never shares a chain with a piped verdict.

## `pgrep -f` / `pkill -f` match the caller

A wait-loop written as

```bash
until ! pgrep -f cargo-mutants; do sleep 15; done
```

can fail to terminate. `-f` matches against the **full command line**,
so whether the caller matches depends on how the loop was invoked:

- **Inline** — `bash -c 'until ! pgrep -f cargo-mutants; …'`, an agent
  or CI one-liner, a `sh -c` wrapper — the pattern is *in* the argv, so
  `pgrep` finds itself and the condition is never false.
- **From a script file** — the argv is the interpreter plus the script
  path, so the pattern normally does **not** appear and the loop
  terminates as intended.

`pkill -f` has the mirror form and the same qualification: from an
inline invocation it can kill the shell that ran it.

Treat this as a hazard of inline and wrapper command lines rather than
a property of every such loop — but note that the inline form is the
one used in agent sessions and CI `run:` blocks, which is where it has
actually bitten.

This is silent and it looks exactly like a slow job, which is what makes
it expensive: the loop sits there consuming its timeout while the thing
it waits for finished long ago. Cost one round a 19-minute stall on a
measurement that had already completed.

Fixes, in order of preference:

- **Wait on the process, not a pattern** — keep the PID (`$!`, or a
  pidfile) and `wait "$pid"`.
- **Match the binary, not the command line** — `pgrep -x <name>`.
- **Exclude the caller** — `pgrep -f <pat> | grep -v "^$$\$"`, which is
  the weakest form because it still depends on the pattern.

The same shape appears whenever a command inspects the process table
that it is itself part of; treat "does my own invocation match this
predicate?" as the first question, not an afterthought.
